**Chapter 757/757**
**Chapter 9: 'The Light of a Nameless Star'**
**Chapter 9 Final Act: 'Reweave'**

―As she watched the servants working at the Barielle manor from a distance, Frederica was struggling with a deeply unsettled feeling.

"It's not uncommon for me to accompany the Master and be treated as a guest... but the way things work in this mansion now is a little different."

Frederica's complaint was directed at the busy state of the mansion reflected in her jade eyes.
She could see that they were an excellent group of personnel, brisk and well-coordinated. Even after being informed of the tragic news that Frederica and the others had brought back, they did not fall into dysfunction. ―That alone was worthy of respect.

"Lady Priscilla seemed to be quite beloved."

The tragic news delivered to the servants―was the report of the death of Priscilla Barielle, the head of the mansion and their master, in the Vollachia Empire.

"――――"

Slightly lowering her eyes, Frederica let out a sigh as she looked down from the balcony at the garden and the mansion.
Due to her position, Frederica was familiar with the feeling of having her masters―starting with Roswaal and Emilia―leave the mansion without her, and being left with no choice but to wait and pray for their safe return. How great must the shock that strikes the heart be when one learns that those prayers went unanswered?
In fact, many of the servants were grieving, and quite a few were shedding tears. The fact that not a single one left the mansion upon learning of the death report was a testament to the relationships Priscilla had built.
However, the reason wasn't just that Priscilla was beloved――

"――It was Master Schult's resolute attitude that struck my heart."

What had deeply impressed Frederica was the demeanor of the young attendant who had accompanied Priscilla to the Empire, bore the role of conveying her death and her will, and accomplished it splendidly.
Schult, who looked slender and as unreliable as a small animal, had not left the role of the hated messenger to Roswaal or Frederica, but had accomplished the great task with his own mouth and words.
That the servants chose not to leave the mansion and continue in their current roles was undoubtedly due in large part to their affection for Priscilla and their trust in Schult. Even Frederica, an outsider, had to agree, having struggled to hold back hot tears at the corners of her eyes.

And so――

"――My, so this is where you were, Frederica. I ended up looking for you-aa."
"Master..."

Greeting the tall figure who raised a hand lightly and stepped onto the balcony――Roswaal, Frederica bent her knee on the spot and gave a small bow.

"My apologies for making you search for me. What was your business?"
"It's not really something I'd call business, thou-agh. Things have settled down a bit on my end, so I just wanted to talk with you a little."
"Settled down..."
"Yes, settled down-aa, indeed."

Roswaal gave a faint smile, closing one eye――the yellow one of his mismatched eyes. He stood next to Frederica by the railing, leaning his back against it.

"It's a land with quite a lot of noteworthy aspects. I have few acquaintances in the south. Sir Leip Barielle, Priscilla-sama's late husband, was not a man with a very good reputation, you see-aa."
"If the Master says so, that man must be quite dissatisfied, I'm sure."
"My, how severe. Have you too been poisoned by Petra-kun and are losing your obedience to me-aa? Or perhaps――"

Roswaal tilted his head and peered into Frederica's eyes beside him.

"――You don't like my plan to use Schult-kun?"
"...I do not believe what the Master does is mistaken. I am aware that it is for the Royal Selection... and ultimately, for Lady Emilia and our camp, after all."
"Even if that's true in theory, it's not easy for the heart to be fully convinced, is it-aa?"

He said this knowing full well, a testament to Roswaal's deeply ingrained bad character. If Emilia were to comment, she'd probably say it was a manifestation of a mischievous spirit that needed a proper scolding.

"That would be difficult in the relationship between the Master and myself, Lady Emilia..."

If Emilia or Petra were here, they could prompt Roswaal to reflect through straightforward means; if Ram were here, she could do it through unorthodox methods. But perhaps anticipating this, only Frederica was present.
And even putting aside the debt of gratitude she could never repay, Frederica understood that Roswaal's actions, as he played the villain, were for the sake of their camp.

――The primary purpose of the Emilia camp remaining united as a camp was, after all, the Royal Selection.

In that Royal Selection, the period spent involved with the Vollachia Empire had put them entirely behind. Even without that, Emilia had started late. To recover from that lap behind and, if possible, gain momentum, they could not rely solely on straightforward methods.
To that end, Roswaal's devoted behind-the-scenes maneuvering was steadily progressing, aiming to bring the Barielle domain, which had just lost its absolute pillar in Priscilla Barielle, to their side.

"Now that Priscilla-sama has passed away, a provisional acting official will be put in place until the next lord is selected. Fortunately, we can put forward a candidate from among those under my influence, and for the role of liaison, we can borrow someone from one of Lyuze-dono's people――preferably from 'Meyers'."
"...Is it not too early to involve those children from the old lady?"
"They say, 'Send the pretty ones on a journey,' don't they? If you keep them cooped up diligently in a birdcage, they will never learn how to spread their wings. I am of the same opinion-aa."

As Roswaal steadily and dispassionately laid out his flawless plan, Frederica genuinely began to suspect that Emilia's assessment of him was true―that he wasn't just a fake villain but a genuine scoundrel.
Probably, it was precisely because he could thoroughly play the role of the villain without any slip-ups that his camp comrades could never truly open their hearts to him. He was too serious.

"Master, if you could at least show a little more consideration――"

She was about to point this out, hoping to prevent this misunderstanding from planting deep doubts not only within their camp but also in the Barielle domain and, ultimately, in Schult's heart.

"――M-M-M-Master Margrave, where are you-aa!?"
"Was that... Master Schult?"

Suddenly, the panicked voice of Schult came from below. Frederica and Roswaal looked at each other. Roswaal, raising an eyebrow, seemed to have no clue either. The master, moving his back from the railing, turned around and called out "Schult-kun" to Schult, who had rushed out into the garden below.
Upon hearing his name called, Schult opened his round eyes wide.

"Master Margrave! I need you to come immediately! There is someone I want you to meet, and there is something I need you to hear-aa!"

△▼△▼△▼△

"――What you have just seen is the will of our late Mistress."

At those words from the red-haired maid who had revealed the contents of the sealed letter――the maid who introduced herself as Yae Tenzen, Roswaal's eyes were wide open, frozen.

"――――"

It was rare to see her master so visibly shaken, but his reaction was not unreasonable. Frederica, who had confirmed the same contents, and everyone else present were also struck with astonishment.
Especially Schult, who was the central figure in the letter's contents, was utterly shocked.
The contents of the letter were――

『――I, Priscilla Barielle, current head of the Barielle family, hereby declare that in naming my formal successor, I change the name of the following individual, Schult, to Schult Barielle, and welcome him as the legitimate child and successor of the Barielle family.』

"Lady Priscilla... me...?"

Schult blinked his eyes at the unexpected event, his face unsure if this was a dream or reality.
But then, hands were placed on his shoulders from behind, and Yae, who had revealed the letter's whereabouts, smiled and rubbed her cheek against the boy, saying, "You're way too surprised, I tell you."

"If you think about how much the Mistress doted on Schult-chan, it's totally plausible, isn't it? ――I think she probably intended to keep it hidden during the Royal Selection, though."
"Lady Yae... Lady Yae, since when did you know about this-aa? You left the mansion months ago and only just came back for me-aa..."

"――." Yae's quiet response came. "I found out just before the Mistress and Schult-chan left for the Empire. She prepared that letter around that time too."

At Yae's quiet answer, large teardrops welled up in Schult's eyes.
Upon inquiry, Yae was a maid who had previously worked at this mansion and had been on leave for a while. Upon hearing the news of Priscilla's death, she had rushed back.
As was evident, Yae seemed to have been close to Schult, and apparently held a position of considerable trust from Priscilla, even being entrusted with the role of guardian for the important letter the lady had left behind.
The fact thus revealed was one that greatly disrupted Roswaal's plans――

"――But, if one thinks of Master Schult, perhaps it is for the best."
"...Frederica?"
"It may differ slightly from the Master's thoughts... but Master Schult and everyone in the mansion should have their hearts saved. ――She truly was an incredible person, wasn't she?"

Despite being a Royal Selection candidate and thus in a position opposed to Emilia, Frederica could only rate Priscilla, with whom she had had almost no contact, as a great personage.
After all――

"To have so thoroughly thwarted the Master's schemes... she must undoubtedly have been a worthy rival for Lady Emilia, mustn't she?"

△▼△▼△▼△

"――――"

While hugging Schult, whose small body was trembling as he looked down at the revealed letter once more, Yae let out a sigh over her own decision to return to the Barielle manor.

――It was all a judgment made to cooperate with the goal Al was aiming for, to help achieve it.

"If there was no signal, I guess that means the plan at the tower failed~"

Originally, Yae was supposed to meet up with Al in the town nearest to the Pleiades Watchtower, cooperate in the mission he had to accomplish, and keep her promise to see him through to his final moments.
However, even when the designated time came, no signal for the rendezvous appeared. Yae judged that Al's plan had failed, withdrew from the nearest town――and promptly returned to the Barielle domain.

"I guess the last time I returned to the mansion openly was after Al-sama stopped the Mistress's assassination..."

That said, in a less conspicuous form, she had actually been entering and leaving the mansion fairly frequently to maintain contact with Al. ――The letter she used as an excuse to return to the mansion was also information she obtained while hiding in the attic during one of those inconspicuous visits.
She had lied to Schult and that scheming noble――Roswaal L. Mathers and his attendant maid, claiming the letter had been entrusted to her by Priscilla.

"Actually, I just saw the Mistress prepare it and stash it in a secret safe."

Because the safe containing the letter was a hidden one, and no one else knew of its existence, Yae was able to reveal its whereabouts and win their trust as someone entrusted with a secret. Using the dead Priscilla for that purpose made her feel somewhat guilty, but――

"――But Mistress, did you really think I wasn't watching?"

Honestly, that was the unclear mystery within Yae for which she had no definite answer.
Priscilla had prepared the letter adopting Schult just before leaving for the Empire and had stored it in the safe around the same time. But Yae, though her servant only for a time, did not underestimate her insight. Had that remarkable woman, with eyes that seemed to see through the hearts of others, truly believed Yae was absent?

『――Use the letter well, make it serve your purpose. In doing so, you may as well save Schult.』

Could it have been Priscilla's consideration to hide the letter in a hidden safe that no one would find unless they turned the mansion upside down―except for Yae, hiding in the attic?
In any case――

"I'll take you up on that, Mistress. ――I have to wait for Al-sama, you see."

Having decided to take advantage of Priscilla's kindness, Yae had returned to the mansion for that purpose.
As mentioned earlier, Al, who had failed to send the signal to continue the plan, had likely failed at the Watchtower and found himself in a situation where executing the plan was difficult. But Al would not die. He was alive.

"Because Al-sama is a monster, after all."

Al would not die; he could not be killed. It was precisely because he was such a monster that Yae followed him.
It was to see the day he achieved his goal, was satisfied, and died―to see the disappearance of the existence she feared. ――For that purpose, she needed to keep Schult at a distance from the Emilia camp.
If anything happened to Al, the Emilia camp, who had accompanied him to the Watchtower, would be involved. She had to be in a position where she could move at any time, in preparation for when he escaped from there.
And so――

"Schult-chan, let's carry on the Mistress's will. It's okay. From now on, I'll also return to the mansion and help you, Schult-chan."
"Lady Yae... Yes. Yes-aa...! I will live up to Lady Priscilla's expectations-aa! I want Lady Yae to help me-aa!"

At the resolve of the honest and cute Schult――no, the boy who had become Schult Bariel, Yae exaggeratedly lowered the corners of her eyes, and the other servants also looked moved, on the verge of tears. The Margrave's attendant maid also wiped a tear from the corner of her eye, touched by his earnestness.
Only the Margrave, whose plans seemed to have gone awry, wore an ambiguous expression, but that seemed like proof that even in death, Priscilla had surpassed someone's schemes, which felt satisfying.
Yes, Priscilla was a master worth serving. ――That much was true.

"I will do my best-aa! I want to show a splendid self when Al-sama and Master Heinkel return-aa!"

However, Yae hesitated how to respond to Schult, whose face was red with enthusiasm.
Yae also shared the hope for Al's return.
But as for Heinkel――

"...About Heinkel-sama... it might be a little difficult for him to return anytime soon~"

△▼△▼△▼△

"――Gwoah!"

Just after the sensation of floating, Heinkel crashed to the ground and let out a low scream.
After groaning for a while, he slowly moved his limbs, undoing the restraints. His wrists and ankles, bound tightly with thin threads, gained freedom, and he checked the feeling of liberation after so long――

"Goddammit...!"

He spat out the words bitterly, as if cursing the misfortune that had befallen him.

――The one who had bound Heinkel and left him in a cabin in the woods where no one visited was the girl who had called herself Yae, an accomplice of Al.
She, with her incomprehensible background of being a former maid who served Priscilla and also an assassin sent after her, had tempted him, saying that if he cooperated with Al, an opportunity to obtain what Heinkel desired――'Dragon's Blood'―would come around. She had tempted him, and yet.

"As soon as there was no contact from that Al bastard, she goes 'the plan failed,' what a damn joke..."

On top of that, when Heinkel demanded an explanation and the plan's continuation, she mercilessly kneed and elbowed him, tied up his nose-bleeding self, and promptly disappeared.
As far as Heinkel was concerned, there was no reason for him to receive such a betrayal, like having the ladder pulled out from under him.

"Every single one of them...!"

The Priscilla he had relied on was dead, the Emilia he tried to rely on next had rejected him, and when he tried to cling to the hand extended by Al, that hand was shaken off and he was dropped into the abyss.
As a result, Heinkel's prospects had reverted to a dead end worse than before the Royal Selection even began.

"But, even so..."

He couldn't give up. He couldn't completely give up. He had never even managed to properly give up on anything.

"――Dammit!"

Staggering to his feet, he wiped the long-dried blood from his nose with his sleeve and started walking. There was no destination for his steps. Even without one, he couldn't stand still.

――Because Heinkel Astrea was a husband and a father.

"Wait for me, Luanna, Reinhard. I... I will..."

Dragging his feet, he placed a hand on the hilt of the sword at his waist, and Heinkel walked on.
There was no destination. ――Only the tattered, rusted prayer he had clung to for many years.

△▼△▼△▼△

"――In the end, it was all hectic and rushed. I'm sorry, Ezzo-san."
"Not at all, it can't be helped. We are also aware that circumstances have changed. In the current situation, I cannot leave this place until replacement personnel arrive, but if necessary, I am ready to testify at any time."
"That helps. ...Truly."

Muttering softly, Subaru bowed his head to Ezzo, who was responding in a gentlemanly manner.
The location was the fifth layer of the Pleiades Watchtower, the large floor facing the great door. There, Subaru――no, Subaru and the others had once again prepared for their journey and were about to depart from the tower at the early dawn, having waited only for the outside to grow light.
Their stay had been a mere half-day; it was an early morning departure.

"Somehow, you guys are always in a rush, aren't you? Can't you ever just calmly return from the tower?"
"She's got a point. Last time we were sent to the Empire, and this time it's this sudden turnaround."
"That's not Subaru's fault! All the blame lies with Al... Geez, I don't even know what to say to Schult-kun... ...That idiot Al."

The ones exchanging these words, the last one added gloomily, were the girls of the return group.
Naturally, each of them had their own thoughts about this situation. About the mere half-day stay, about having to leave in such a hurry, and about the cause that led to it.
But for Subaru, it was painful to see the person who was the cause being constantly condemned.

"Boss, I loaded the luggage onto the dragon carriage. We can leave anytime-zz."

At that moment, Garfiel, who had slightly opened the great door and returned from outside, called out. Having loaded the luggage onto the dragon carriage ahead of the others, he frowned at Subaru, who raised his hand.

"You're makin' a stingy face, Boss. It's like 'Lauraage's Hangnail,' I get ya wanna worry 'bout it, but-zz."
"Yeah, I get you're tryin' to cheer me up, but it's not clickin'. Hangnail? Lauraage?"
"Anywa-zz, forget it! Can't change what's already happened-zz. So, it's 'bout how we deal with things from here on out, right? I'll help with anythin'-zz."

Garfiel grinned, showing his fangs, and slapped Subaru's shoulder powerfully.
The force made Subaru's frail shoulder feel like it might break, but his heart felt a hundred times happier. And, as Garfiel said――what was important was what came next, now that they had decided to act.

"――I'll properly find out. Just what it is I've taken on."

Saying this, Subaru touched the magic jewel hanging from his neck and declared his resolve.

――He and Beatrice had been sealed inside a jewel just like this one, and during that time, he couldn't clearly know the details of the great, great battle that Al had set in motion.

"――――"

Inside the magic jewel, it was dark, he couldn't move, the state of his consciousness was ambiguous; it was like returning to being a fetus, floating in amniotic fluid inside a mother's womb, a constant dreamlike state.
There, Subaru's dreams and reality were blurred, and nothing was certain. The ever-diligent Beatrice had been valiantly working on analyzing the forbidden art that sealed them and dismantling the spell formula, but all Subaru could do when he had something resembling consciousness was activate the Authority of Greed――'Cor Leonis' to sense the presence of his nearby comrades and pick up on strong emotional fluctuations.
Even so, thanks to his longing for his comrades, his desperation, and the repeated, extreme ups and downs of powerful emotions, he had been able to continuously feel the progression of extraordinary events with his soul.
Particularly distinctive were Petra's emotions, colored by intense resolve and determination, and Rem's feelings towards Subaru, which could only be described as massive and inflated. ――Because of that, Subaru was able to propose the policy of departing from the tower at dawn with conviction.
It was also because their original purpose for visiting the Pleiades Watchtower had been for Al's sake, and now that they knew it was his pretext, there was no remaining reason to stay, but not only that――

"――There is a way to make the Archbishop of Gluttony properly vomit up the things he's eaten, right?"
"That's right. Among them are the... 'memories' and 'name' of everyone eaten so far, including Rem's."

At Petra's words―Petra, who in the loop before the last 'Return by Death' had probably utilized the Authority of Gluttony most effectively in their strategy―Subaru nodded deeply.

In that instant, that moment when they couldn't even exchange words, Rem, who had looked at Subaru with eyes that understood everything and slammed the Morning Star into him. ――She, who wielded the spiked iron ball Subaru polished every day with such grace, had indeed regained herself. That was undoubtedly due to the involvement of Gluttony.
With that conviction, and with Gluttony already in their custody, there was no reason not to press him until he vomited up everything he had eaten.
And so, to have that direct negotiation――

"――Let's go, to the nostalgic Royal Capital! We'll meet Gluttony and make him spit everything out! And also, it seems I can't afford to be indifferent to the history of the Kingdom, or the history of this world."

Thinking of Al, sealed within the magic jewel he held between his fingers, Subaru admonished himself.
In response to Subaru's declaration of resolve, Beatrice, standing beside him, held his hand; Garfiel stroked his fang with a finger; and Meili shrugged her shoulders where the small red scorpion rested.
And Subaru accepted the gaze of Petra, who smiled with a brave and dignified smile.

For Aldebaran, for the one who called himself the 'Star that Chases After Subaru,' who opposed him using the same power, and whom he had decided to save without fail, Natsuki Subaru was heading for the Royal Capital.
He would learn. Because he had realized that remaining ignorant was insincere, and that he could no longer forgive himself for it.

And so――

"――Bring it on, Fate!"

Bearing his thoughts, declaring he would step over the curses that stood in his way, Natsuki Subaru made his proclamation.

△▼△▼△▼△

――At the very same moment as Natsuki Subaru's declaration of resolve.

In a land far, far away, at the destination Natsuki Subaru had declared he would depart for―the Royal Capital of the Dragonfriend Kingdom of Lugunica―a certain unusual event was taking place.

"――――"

In the early dawn, when the majority of the royal capital's people were still asleep, missing the arrival of morning within their dreams, an unusual atmosphere hung over Lugunica's royal castle, which should normally be wrapped in silence.

The source of that atmosphere was the center of the royal castle――the throne room.

Due to the extinction of the royal family from illness, which had also been the trigger for the Royal Selection, the throne room in Lugunica Castle had been firmly closed except for the holding of important ceremonies.
However, on this day, this morning, the doors to the throne room were wide open, and there gathered the kingdom's dignitaries――members of the 'Council of Wise Men', vassels who had been trusted by the late king, and even kingdom soldiers carrying an air of solemn tension.

"――――"

They were all individuals who bore heavy responsibilities in the kingdom in the king's absence.
There was not a single person who was late, even for this early morning summons, nor was there any insolent individual who showed sleepiness or a lack of concentration in their attitude.
Therefore, regardless of the time of day, the weight of the attention focused here could be said to be that of the kingdom itself.
――And the woman bore that attention entirely upon herself, while maintaining a serene atmosphere.

"――――"

Kneeling on one knee on the marble floor covered with a red carpet was a figure clad in indigo robes, hands clasped before her chest, head bowed. That praying posture was so refined that military officials were reminded of martial skill, and civil officials of etiquette, making them inadvertently sigh.
To such an extent, with just the gesture of praying, the woman overwhelmed her surroundings.

"――Fuum. Would you be so kind as to... raise your face?"

Amidst this, there was only one person who was not captivated by the woman's praying gesture and spoke up.
The owner of the voice, gentle and intellectual, naturally imparting such an impression to those who heard it, was one of the castle's personnel who had received the kneeling girl――no, one could say their representative.

Miklotov McMahon.――In the Lugunica Kingdom, where there was no king, he was the central figure of the 'Council of Wise Men' who steered affairs of state, and even in this hastily arranged setting, he held the greatest voice and power of decision.
The wise man, known for his outstanding intellect and unparalleled loyalty and contribution to the kingdom, was such a significant presence that the king himself had once jokingly said, "Even if the King of Lugunica dies, the country will not waver, but if he dies, the kingdom will fall."
His gentle appearance and mild manner of speaking could lead to misunderstandings, but in truth, he was a mastermind said to be the most reliable if made an ally, and the most fearsome if made an enemy in the Lugunica Kingdom.――Miklotov was a man who had enjoyed such evaluations for many years.

"――Yes."

Accepting Miklotov's words, the woman slowly raised her face. And when her closed eyelids opened, Miklotov, who met her gaze, faintly caught his breath.
That reaction of Miklotov's was not his alone. Bordeaux Zellgef, also present as a member of the 'Council of Wise Men', Marcos Gildark, the Captain of the Royal Guard standing here as the representative of the military officials, Rickert Hoffman, the central figure among the civil officials, and all others present in the throne room at that moment, each reacted.

A woman with a demonic nature that naturally drew everyone's attention just by being there. However, everyone's reaction at this time was not solely due to her bearing.――It was her appearance.
Her long, lustrous golden hair and crimson eyes, harboring a strong, dignified will, pierced those present here――no, the people of the Lugunica Kingdom―with more than just beauty.
And then――

"――First of all, I thank you for this sudden visit and for kindly welcoming me. Considering our respective positions, it must not have been desirable."
"Fuum, you say rather difficult things. That said, there's no point in pretense. What you say is fact, after all."
"To have you say that so frankly takes a load off my shoulders. Being a novice like myself, I was afraid I wouldn't be able to find my words before everyone."
"Fuum, you say that, yet you seem quite composed. You seem accustomed to attracting public attention. I imagine... you have no small number of such experiences."
"Yes, I won't deny that."

Though there was an age difference akin to that between grandfather and granddaughter, the exchange between Miklotov and the woman was fluid, and it didn't seem like it would become a one-sided probing due to the difference in their roles.
Both parties, seemingly having assessed this, simultaneously smiled as if letting out a breath.

"I cannot idly take up the time of you busy people so early in the morning. So, if I may... let us get to the main point."
"Fuum, that would be best. At my age, waking early isn't a hardship, but important matters are painful to postpone no matter how old one gets. ――Especially when they come from the 'Dragon Sanctuary,' which has kept its distance from the royal castle."
"――――"

Although he had urged her on magnanimously, the atmosphere in the room tightened for an instant at Miklotov's statement. The addition was immediately softened, deemed discourteous to the woman who had come to the castle alone, but the reason for it was something no one present could do anything about.

――The 'Dragon Sanctuary,' named by Miklotov, was, as the name implied, a religious organization that revered the existence of the 'Dragon God' Volcanica and believed in the blessings of its power and the covenant of protection.

As the name 'Dragonfriend Kingdom' suggested, Lugunica was known for its special, deep connection with the 'Dragon,' and most people of the kingdom, to a greater or lesser extent, shared in the 'Dragon God's' benevolence.
Aware of this, even if not officially affiliated with the church, the majority of Lugunica's citizens held a sense of reverence for the 'Dragon God.' In such an environment, the depth of 'Dragon God' faith held by those officially affiliated with the church went without saying.
To prevent the nation from being shaken by its immense influence, the 'Dragon Sanctuary' did not approach the royal castle that managed state affairs, and they contributed to the people's happiness within their respective domains.――That was the tacit understanding.
For that very reason, a shrine maiden of the 'Dragon Sanctuary' visiting the royal castle and attempting to speak before those including the 'Council of Wise Men' was an extraordinary event.
However――

"――It is worth it."

Precisely because she understood it was extraordinary, there was a reason worthy of breaking that unwritten rule.
The woman's assertion performed the role of making them expect that it indeed had the value of gathering Miklotov and the others in the throne room to hear her out.
Therefore, following the silent urging of the wise men, the woman announced.――The reason for her visit.

"Regarding the kingdom's recent perils, the faithful of the 'Dragon Sanctuary' are heartily concerned. By tacit understanding, we have kept our distance from the state matter of the Royal Selection... but ever since word of the calamity in the watergate city reached our ears, we could no longer stand by idly."
"The watergate city... Pri Stella, the Witch Cult incident, I presume. We too are pained by the damage to that city, but could it be the 'Dragon Sanctuary' has some complaint against the Royal Selection candidates involved in the incident?"
"If that were the case, it would contradict the meaning of me rushing here even at the cost of breaking the tacit understanding, and my saying there was value in hearing me out. ...It would be a contradiction."

A slip of the tongue in her immediate rebuttal showed her true self, and she corrected herself. That bit of awkwardness revealed the woman's youth. As if to cover her embarrassment, she gave a small cough.

"Anyway, I have no complaints with the Royal Selection candidates. It's not that there aren't some individuals I might have a thing or two to say to, but that is not the consensus of the 'Dragon Sanctuary,' but personal opinion."
"If it is not the church's official view, then it is a candid opinion as a citizen of the kingdom. Rather, I believe we should listen actively... but fuum, that digresses from the topic."
"Yes. I'll remember that you are someone I can speak to, Lord Miklotov, but to the main point. ――What we wish to discuss is not the assignment of responsibility, but the victims."
"――. Let us hear it."
"Regarding those who fell victim to the Witch Cult in the watergate city of Pri Stella... I hear multiple cases have been confirmed. The 'Dragon Sanctuary' can provide a means of salvation for them."

――Instantly, the air in the throne room shook greatly at the meaning of those words.
However, the woman who was the epicenter of that tremor maintained her dignified posture, continuing to stand straight under the gaze of the wise men. Her gaze and attitude showed no wavering; there was a self-confidence that could not be declared without conviction.

"――Fuum."

With that single word of acknowledgment, Miklotov severed the murmuring air.
That said, even Miklotov, one of the kingdom's foremost wise men, could not hide his surprise at her offer. The wise man showed a hint of busyness in the fingers stroking his long, long beard.

"If that is true, it would be a most welcome story. That you brought this matter to the royal castle is..."
"If we were to proceed on our own, we wouldn't be looked upon favorably. And besides..."
"Those who wouldn't look favorably are not just us of the castle, but also the Witch Cult, I presume."

Needless to say, the woman also pulled in her slender chin in agreement with Miklotov.
Naturally, if the damage caused by the Witch Cult could be undone, it would be nothing but an undesirable event for the Witch Cult. That was precisely why the 'Dragon Sanctuary' desired it.

"――The kingdom's certain protection. Due to the distance between us, who govern the state, and the 'Dragon Sanctuary,' which governs faith, that has not been realized in the true sense for many years, has it?"
"Yes, that's right. We wish to realize it on this occasion. That is the consensus of the 'Dragon Sanctuary'. ――To give that decision a final push, we have a proposal from our side."
"Fuum, a proposal? And that is?"
"First, allow us to show you. The salvation we can provide, its proof."

Placing a hand on her own chest as she said this, Miklotov raised his eyebrows at the woman.
It was a more than welcome offer. As she said, it was an impeccable proposal as a basis for judgment. However, bringing the victims from the watergate city to the royal capital immediately would be――

"There should be one person you have in mind. The first one should be that person."
"――. Very well. I grant you permission to first make the approach. ――Captain Marcos."
"Sir!"

Called by name by Miklotov, the giant in armor responded with a booming voice. The captain of the Royal Guard, Marcos, stepped forward and saluted the seats of the 'Council of Wise Men'.

"Please escort her. This will become a major affair for the kingdom. Proceed with caution."
"Understood. ――I shall accompany you personally."
"Yes, a big face... no, a person with a large physique. Thank you."

The woman bowed her head, and a certain softness also dwelled in her expression. Perhaps it was because the flow of the conversation was proceeding as she had hoped, and it seemed she could fulfill the meaning of her visit from the 'Dragon Sanctuary'.
To her, knowing it might seem like a surprise attack, Miklotov asked about something.
About the reason this woman, who called herself an envoy from the 'Dragon Sanctuary' and gathered the kingdom's dignitaries at the castle from the early morning, attracted attention beyond merely being a member of the 'Dragon Sanctuary'.
That was――

"――A great discourtesy, but might you tell us your name, Shrine Maiden-dono?"

△▼△▼△▼△

"――I am a shrine maiden dispatched from the 'Dragon Sanctuary'."

Facing the woman who had been brought to the noble villa by Marcos himself――the one who called herself a shrine maiden, Ferris was unable to hide his confusion.

The reason Ferris was staying in the royal capital with Wilhelm was to make a direct appeal to the royal castle――to participate as a member of the investigation team to the opened Pleiades Watchtower, and to ask the all-knowing, all-powerful 'Sage' for a means to save Crusch, who continued to suffer.
Of course, they had already received a letter from Emilia and the others who had returned from the Pleiades Watchtower, informing them that the existence of the 'Sage' and his wisdom were not as convenient as hoped. But even so, it held more promise than the slow progress of healing magic development.

"Really, it's ridiculous. No matter how much I improve existing healing magic, it only makes it so I don't die myself, and doesn't remove a single one of Crusch-sama's pains."

The desperate research of the 'Blue' had raised the level of existing healing magic in the Lugunica Kingdom by not one, but three stages in this short period, but to the current Ferris, that was worthless.
The one person he wanted to save most right now, he couldn't save. That fact tormented Ferris, casting him into darkness.
And so, he had tried to rely on the Pleiades Watchtower, on the Sage's wisdom, but――

"...You can save her? Crusch-sama? The 'Dragon Sanctuary' can? R-really?"
"We do not lie in our faith, and we have no taste for further tormenting those who are lost. Even if you cannot believe me, your Captain should be believable."

Responding to Ferris's trembling voice, the shrine maiden gestured with her hand towards the accompanying Marcos. When Ferris called out "Captain!" in a high-pitched voice to him, he nodded with a face like a rock.

"I have heard that the 'Dragon Sanctuary' possesses magic and techniques not made public. The members of the 'Council of Wise Men,' starting with Lord Miklotov, also seem to think it has value enough to believe in."
"...B-but, what if something happens to Crusch-sama again?"
"――'The Stone of Kwein cannot be lifted alone.'"
"――!"

At the shrine maiden's quietly spoken words, Ferris inadvertently gasped.
As turbulent emotions welled up, moistening Ferris's eyes, the shrine maiden gently reached for his hand, softly loosening the tightly clenched fingers one by one.
Ferris, allowing this to happen, suddenly noticed her beautiful golden hair and crimson eyes filled with a serious light, and stared wide-eyed.
The realization only added to his confusion―he didn't understand what it meant―

"Please. Do allow us to help your precious person. The 'Dragon Sanctuary' always wishes for the happiness of the kingdom's people. No matter what principles one holds, there are no exceptions there."
"Y-you... who are you?"

"――――"

At that question from Ferris, she held her breath. However, she gathered her slight hesitation with a blink and, still softly holding Ferris's hand, answered.
That was――

"――Fourier. That is my name."

――Fourier. The shrine maiden with the golden hair and crimson eyes characteristic of the royal family gave the same name as the princess who had been taken from the royal castle fifteen years ago and had disappeared.

